      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T14:44:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t disagree with anything Brian S said, it does seem like names can be addressed to have this be a verb instead of a noun.\n\nHowever, if we\u0027re trying to address the `sample` texture confusion, maybe we switch to something like `eval`? That fits my mental model that we\u0027re invoking a function that was passed in as an argument (via uniform).",
      "parentUuid": "36e2cb84_490a9569",
      "revId": "7c934cb85d8787a83424f9285bbf3d18d969c82e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84b92387_88d95d2c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T15:16:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s not difficult for us to have multiple intrinsics here, if it makes the language clearer. If we use one function name to mean both \"sample from a texture\" and \"call a function\" I suspect we are just going to shift the confusion point around.\n\n(`sample` implies that every child FP is as cheap as a texture read. `eval` implies they are all as expensive as evaluating the result of a function call. In reality it depends on what we are sampling-from/evaluating.)",
      "parentUuid": "3c3e375b_c4bc3798",
      "revId": "7c934cb85d8787a83424f9285bbf3d18d969c82e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "64e9cee1_7c28b72e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T15:25:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I had also thought about eval(), although I\u0027m starting to wonder if we just fix this with naming instead. HLSL has the object/method-call pattern, but that\u0027s pretty foreign to GLSL. (Other than array.count(), for some reason). The sampler pattern is still to use sample(), plus other intrinsics that operate on sampler objects (eg, to get texture dimensions).\n\nThat, plus the fact that any further operations are still theoretical, makes me lean back towards the current approach (directly invoking the child objects with function call syntax). Of all the options, that most directly reflects the underlying mechanism, too.",
      "parentUuid": "84b92387_88d95d2c",
      "revId": "7c934cb85d8787a83424f9285bbf3d18d969c82e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "01c3b8d7_60a04541",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T19:43:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Talked with Brian some more, and I\u0027m now in favor of yet-another alternative:\n\n  - Keep invocation as intrinsic functions, but rename them from sample() to shade/filter/blend (depending on the type).\n\nThis is a smaller syntactic substitution for clients. It more closely aligns with GLSL idioms, while also using type-specific verbs that don\u0027t suggest texture-sampling. If we ever allow binding of an actual SkImage (rather than SkShader), we can use sample(), and it remains consistent with both GLSL, and the pattern we\u0027d be establishing for other Skia types.",
      "parentUuid": "64e9cee1_7c28b72e",
      "revId": "7c934cb85d8787a83424f9285bbf3d18d969c82e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    }
